    {
      "id": "yeats-collected-poems-s0499-p01086",
      "source_en": "You ask what I have found and far and wide I go, 304\nYou gave, but will not give again 107\nYou say, as I have often given tongue 94\nYou think it horrible that lust and rage 3 12\nYou waves, though you dance by my feet like children at\nplay, 21\nYou who are bent, and bald, and blind, 355\n'Your eyes that once were never weary of mine 15\nYour hooves have stamped at the black margin of the\nwood, 215",
